<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>mux.Handle("GET /assets/", http.StripPrefix("/assets/",
    http.FileServer(http.Dir("/srv/my-site/assets")),
))</code></pre>
<p>This serves the files prepared under <code>/srv/my-site/assets</code> at <code>/assets/</code>. The file server needs the filesystem directory, while generated imports use browser paths. An absolute filesystem path prevents a different server working directory from changing which files are served.</p>
<h2>Browser bootstrap</h2>
<pre><code>// assets/app.js
import htmx from "htmx";
window.htmx = htmx;</code></pre>
<pre><code>&lt;script type="module" src="/assets/app.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Include the prepared import map before this script. The <code>window</code> assignment is useful for application code that expects the HTMX global; it is an explicit choice in the bootstrap, not something go-importmap generates.</p>
<h2>Embedded assets</h2>
<pre><code>//go:embed assets
var assets embed.FS

public, err := fs.Sub(assets, "assets")
if err != nil { return err }
mux.Handle("GET /assets/", http.StripPrefix("/assets/", http.FileServer(http.FS(public))))</code></pre>
<p>Prepare downloaded files before building the binary so the embed directive can include them. Custom scripts, stylesheet sources and images are application-owned assets; keep them in the public tree or mount them deliberately.</p>
<h2>Full pages and fragments</h2>
<p>Place the dependency head in the full-page layout. A go-partial fragment normally updates content inside the already loaded page, so it does not need another copy of the import map or module bootstrap.</p>
</article>
